{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgQ4L3cpBXmCa2cCot1rs","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"U.S. Steel's inventory turnover for FY2024 was about **6.55 times**, or roughly 56 days of inventory on hand.\n\n**Calculation** (inventory turnover = cost of goods sold ÷ average inventory)\n\n| Item | Amount |\n|---|---|\n| Cost of goods sold, FY2024 | $14,060M |\n| Inventory, 31 Dec 2023 | $2,128M |\n| Inventory, 31 Dec 2024 | $2,168M |\n| Average inventory: (2,128 + 2,168) ÷ 2 | $2,148M |\n| **Inventory turnover: 14,060 ÷ 2,148** | **≈ 6.55x** |\n| Days inventory outstanding: 365 ÷ 6.55 | ≈ 55.8 days |\n\n- If you use year-end inventory instead of the average, turnover is 14,060 ÷ 2,168 ≈ 6.49x.\n- For comparison, FY2023 turnover was about 7.0x: COGS of $15,803M ÷ average inventory of $2,243.5M, from $2,359M and $2,128M. So stock moved somewhat more slowly in 2024, mainly because COGS fell.\n- U.S. Steel no longer has a current ticker in SEC's list, so I looked it up by its company ID number (CIK 1163302).\n- All figures come from the XBRL data filed with the 10-K. The inventory figure is net of valuation and LIFO reserves.\n\n**Sources**\n- U.S. Steel Form 10-K for FY2024, filed 2025-01-31, accession 0001163302-25-000018 (FY2024 COGS; inventory at 31 Dec 2024 and 31 Dec 2023; FY2023 COGS)\n- U.S. Steel Form 10-K for FY2023, filed 2024-02-02, accession 0001163302-24-000009 (inventory at 31 Dec 2022, for the FY2023 comparison)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2232,"cache_read_input_tokens":6905,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2232},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"81018054-5ec8-401d-943b-be455